Chaining &lt;&lt;
std::cout &lt;&lt; "v1=" &lt;&lt; v1 &lt;&lt; " v2=" &lt;&lt; v2 &lt;&lt; std::endl;
// is evaluated as
((((std::cout &lt;&lt; "v1=") &lt;&lt; v1) &lt;&lt; " v2=") &lt;&lt; v2) &lt;&lt; std::endl;
// output: v1=(1,2,3) v2=(0.5,-1,2)

&lt;&lt; groups from left to right (left associativity): the leftmost &lt;&lt; is done first, and its result is the left operand of the next one.
So every &lt;&lt; must return the stream itself, std::cout: return os;, type std::ostream&. The &lt;&lt; of the standard library do the same: ours fits in the chain.
By reference: a stream cannot be copied. With std::ostream (by value): error: use of deleted function 'std::basic_ostream&lt;...&gt;::basic_ostream(const std::basic_ostream&lt;...&gt;&)'.
With void, the chain breaks at the next &lt;&lt;: error: invalid operands of types 'void' and 'const char [2]' to binary 'operator&lt;&lt;'.
Compare with =, which groups from right to left: A = B = C is A = (B = C) (Lab #2). Both return a reference, used by the next operator.

